#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228848 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228849 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228850 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228854 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9519108d-63fa-5301-9f54-9c07cc57a3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238167} {:answer/id #uuid "7d54c2b7-2fd4-5ac6-9b17-e3e1e75fe048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9519108d-63fa-5301-9f54-9c07cc57a3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238167} :answer-field/id #uuid "c347185c-c6be-5fc9-98f1-bc6eada3c21b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de7735d2-3904-5b74-a25a-790295b495b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332398" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5d45f45-d2d6-5aeb-a3eb-93aee8306745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^x+2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5d45f45-d2d6-5aeb-a3eb-93aee8306745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^x+2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238172} :answer-field/id #uuid "46a85e34-2524-5490-9448-b932b3b78a02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9119bbb7-05c6-5b96-825f-1b48a35651df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332531" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e462e1-4c24-599b-9784-bc6a819f34bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e462e1-4c24-599b-9784-bc6a819f34bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378507} :answer-field/id #uuid "b442ff4e-d61d-572e-aa1f-a0e72d9b120b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8903add6-0b71-512c-a68b-c77444eff598" :question/math-academy-id "q-332644" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=9{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56622ace-6fee-5f7e-9817-a3fb4b9d29eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56622ace-6fee-5f7e-9817-a3fb4b9d29eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378510} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f497ed-1344-5460-9b0a-6f4b2b8a164a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07b0d75a-b632-5a31-b7e2-32353413f5df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332659" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-18y=24{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $24{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})-18(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-12-18)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -30\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e63b12a-6ceb-51f9-a630-adac55902e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}e^x+\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301948} {:answer/id #uuid "0086491b-8f28-5d3f-b14a-87b1827fd9ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}e^{x}-\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e63b12a-6ceb-51f9-a630-adac55902e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}e^x+\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301948} :answer-field/id #uuid "39a18d23-5024-5935-8c97-6b1c447792ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b00adf-08fa-5ac3-9680-3e510439c0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332668" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x})+9(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}) & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3\\alpha +9\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(12\\beta +9\\beta ){e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 12\\alpha {e}^{x}+21\\beta {e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{4x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n12\\alpha =7\\,\\text{and}\\,21\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{7}{12}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{21}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e49c025-3cae-5a35-a78c-63f2aa273051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}-\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378514} {:answer/id #uuid "332865e6-9a5b-5d5c-b86b-117ec5da9654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "332865e6-9a5b-5d5c-b86b-117ec5da9654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378515} :answer-field/id #uuid "313afa76-69ee-5070-9624-01f055352dba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aba6e3a2-6b9c-5da4-a0fb-f7b0bbffa2ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-332669" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ (2\\alpha +3\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(5\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x}+8\\beta {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{5x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n5\\alpha =2,\\,8\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bcc7e2-88f7-5ef9-8c51-77c1e2c8e8af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ded373d6-e3a7-5ae2-bff6-4cb4e46263ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}e^x+e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded373d6-e3a7-5ae2-bff6-4cb4e46263ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}e^x+e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301951} :answer-field/id #uuid "72563d45-b8a2-5c79-ac74-8ffbb1b1368b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ed16f1e-5ea8-5e61-9dde-90ceef762829" :question/math-academy-id "q-332670" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(4\\beta -6\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{x}-2\\beta {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{2x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha =3,\\,\\,-2\\beta =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b39559e-dadc-5abc-a8e3-a5022294e4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b39559e-dadc-5abc-a8e3-a5022294e4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378518} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57619ce-03f2-5496-8140-e8c0883afbc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b63cbf2-f8fc-50ae-8f0d-7e9e3508d562" :question/math-academy-id "q-332686" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x})+4\\alpha x{e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x} & =8{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eac0720-d094-5c11-a613-f13f3aea6fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eac0720-d094-5c11-a613-f13f3aea6fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301954} :answer-field/id #uuid "774e0589-6fc8-53b4-90b8-7d9ab66d7716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "628da0cc-0f9a-5282-9f1d-c8b6b2f1d84a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332728" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45618579-1a6b-55d0-9e61-d7f16d3e0635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}-2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301963} {:answer/id #uuid "513b0bbd-042d-5c0a-a8a9-6fa2f0bbc620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}+2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513b0bbd-042d-5c0a-a8a9-6fa2f0bbc620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xe^{-3x}+2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301964} :answer-field/id #uuid "15624438-d31e-5ef4-a455-9dfaf4cf1ce5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f7c308b-95b8-5ee7-9e1d-2fe096f6d411" :question/math-academy-id "q-332795" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(2\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =10,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee59641-63fe-5422-ac53-afc2d3bf4ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{x}+5e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378521} {:answer/id #uuid "8def97b9-9fed-5af8-9fd6-823fc752faa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{x}-5e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee59641-63fe-5422-ac53-afc2d3bf4ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{x}+5e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378521} :answer-field/id #uuid "690db9fd-5d56-5bdb-8177-c16979aaa76a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb257cc1-ec43-5317-b99b-94cdb5d3aa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332801" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=20{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $20{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b42f88-262c-5a8a-b682-d6673316f11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12xe^{-4x}-\\frac{8}{5}e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378525} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0c4c76-5dfc-5f85-8d61-d54e89d0b5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12xe^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e0c4c76-5dfc-5f85-8d61-d54e89d0b5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12xe^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378526} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a94788-eef2-5bc4-a13f-df483b975efc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a0dd5a1-7136-5b94-ab63-02aa4707dd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-332848" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =8,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38dede99-f661-51a4-8cb0-0d8ab909dbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{2x}+5xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301971} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9800da-a179-5890-a4ee-f7b40850a699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{2x}+5e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301972} {:answer/id #uuid "50b49da0-5c75-570b-8b8e-d5c171bbfa66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{2x}-5xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38dede99-f661-51a4-8cb0-0d8ab909dbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{2x}+5xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301971} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6be2b9-32ff-58c2-bd7b-c9805bf5f3af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30b74f30-6c22-5b7d-8b5b-0cd838e6fa68" :question/math-academy-id "q-332895" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "990cf1be-b2ee-5d1a-93ec-81c6c5845c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+4e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301975} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7e1c04-4c4a-509d-824e-0d678d0629b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+4xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad7e1c04-4c4a-509d-824e-0d678d0629b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+4xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301976} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4006666-11b2-57a3-9efc-38fe316e73f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4446c513-dd4a-5bfc-a354-84536434cfee" :question/math-academy-id "q-332898" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b852c8-03bb-5bac-b58e-3d6ca9d1263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}e^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b852c8-03bb-5bac-b58e-3d6ca9d1263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}e^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378530} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af76db-ff80-515a-9f26-3a346ee89b0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "835ac161-f259-59c7-85ca-dd6f39fecf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-332908" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=10\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{4x}-\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ 3\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=10$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =10 \\\\ A & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c15459d-03a2-598e-9950-72958fea26ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-2x}+5e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301992} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bc8efc-f29b-54c5-9632-3288541ed307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-2x}-5e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c15459d-03a2-598e-9950-72958fea26ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{-2x}+5e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301992} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff774b99-6268-59bc-b6fa-6cefd366084f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9176374c-4880-5679-b51e-bd8fad9c53cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332909" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =8 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b85a9f66-3027-5f91-9326-7620d080a26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}-2xe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378533} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce082ac-27c8-5218-b64d-66392d740147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}+2xe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85a9f66-3027-5f91-9326-7620d080a26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}-2xe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378533} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb0b9f0a-28e4-5022-9c32-cc06ecdfc3da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ffa1d9c-35b7-510b-b9ab-e033fffbefc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332937" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{4x}) & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2c35bf-27c4-5a62-b2cd-6d52b03bce70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378537} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3c54d9-bc5d-583d-8987-a2cb5bbe62ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b3c54d9-bc5d-583d-8987-a2cb5bbe62ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10xe^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8b826b9-8f4c-5074-98ca-03a81079183c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e7c5bfe-a697-5dc4-aa2f-68c1dae5b4f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332940" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2(\\alpha x{e}^{2x}) & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =0 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba81125d-c905-5b57-af0b-715c31a4a497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x+1)e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba81125d-c905-5b57-af0b-715c31a4a497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x+1)e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325197} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fe8f3a-0956-532c-8a2f-0ee306a9271f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "002b88e2-78e7-53d4-907a-a035eccfda11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333160" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}) & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8172e3fa-619d-5db8-b804-f1808d945c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{5x}-e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8172e3fa-619d-5db8-b804-f1808d945c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{5x}-e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378541} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfda6193-452b-5d2b-8c25-5d33498e04d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ebb7209-c4d1-5ca8-9307-dd4f5c48183e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333176" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=2{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-5\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}]}
